* CED_EDIT
* The data collection editor (openQM CED and !CED(), 1.2-0).  This is the full-screen host: it owns the screen, paints, reads the keys and writes the record.  What the editor does with
* the data is gpl.bp/ced_core (the engine, which the IDE also uses); the CED command (gpl.bp/ced) and the !CED() subroutine (gpl.bp/ced_sub) call this.
* Copyright (c) 2026, SD Core for Linux.  This program is free software; you can redistribute
* it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 30 Oct 26 dm  The one-editor plan, stage 1 (no change in what the editor does): everything between the key loop and the painting is gpl.bp/ced_core now, with its state in a collection;
*               this program keeps the screen, the key loop, the break setting, the layout and the write of the record (with the edit gate), and says so to the engine.
* 28 Oct 26 dm  The break setting the editor takes is kept by $tk_brk (save, and close at both exits), so that an abort inside the editor does not leave Ctrl-C dead (TUI phase 2 review).
* 26 Oct 26 dm  Data mode (ftype 2, the one-editor plan, stage 1): c is a record as $recarr makes it, an array of fields; only text and lists of values and subvalues can be made;
*               Ctrl-S makes the record text with $recarr, and writes it when id is not null.  Used by the IDE's "Edit as fields" (F12).
* 18 Oct 26 dm  Terminal 140 by 42 (plan-terminal-140x42.md, phase 3).  The editor is laid out for at least 140 by 42 and refuses a
*               smaller window at the start with the library's one message ($sl_need, 12202; the result tells a caller with more
*               records to stop).  A window made smaller after the start shows the upper left corner of the layout (read.layout:
*               the layout never shrinks below 140 by 42), and while the selected row is off the screen $sl_key takes the keyboard
*               ($sl_guardmode); Ctrl-Q, y there leaves without keeping anything (the key guardquit).
* 18 Oct 26 dm  S.66: the refusal text comes from !edit_msg, which also knows the shared account's two refusals (12518, 12519).
* 17 Oct 26 dm  S.64: save.record asks the kernel (K$EDIT.OK) before it writes into a VOC, a dictionary or a program file, and says why in
*               the message line when the session has not the gate.
* 24 Oct 26 dm  AI line stage 3: F2, when the AI is usable for the account, opens a menu (explain the selected element, ask about the record, undo the AI's last change).  The model
*               sees the record as indented JSON; edits it proposes are shown as a diff and, if accepted and valid JSON, replace c (marked changed; nothing is saved).
* 12 Oct 26 dm  New.  Written from the openQM pages for CED and !CED(); the keys are theirs, the
*               layout and the messages are SD's own.
* 13 Oct 26 dm  On the screen library (owner: "use the TUI on ced and debugger"): the element list is a
*               table widget, the questions are dialogues (SCREEN ASK, MSGBOX, MENU), the keys are the
*               library's names.  What the editor does with the data is as it was.
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.181 (the owner, 9 Nov: "lift the freeze so it uses the shared keys"): the keys every full-screen page shares (S.179) - Ctrl-T the next theme, kept for the
*               person; Ctrl-K the keys of this screen; F5 and Ctrl-L draw it again - through $tk_std, from a key table ck that names every key the editor takes.
*               Copy, cut and paste are Ctrl-C, X and V only, and Alt-T changes the type as F4 does (the engine).
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.134: the editor asks $sl_guardmode for the LAYOUT mode, as the IDE does, so the keyboard is held while the window is smaller than LAYW by LAYH
*               and not only while the cursor is off the screen: at 120 by 36 the editor redrew and took keys; it now writes the lock message and takes none.
* 10 Nov 26 dm S.193: laid out for 140 by 43, the standing full-screen size (LAYH was 42).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $cedit(c, fv, id, ftype, title, result)
*
*     c       the collection or array to edit.  It is edited in place; if the changes are not
*             kept it is put back as it was when the editor was called.
*     fv      the file variable the record is to be saved to (any value when id is null)
*     id      the record id; null for a plain variable, when Ctrl-S and a saving quit keep the
*             changes in c and write nothing
*     ftype   0 if the file is a data collection file (the collection is written), 1 if it is
*             an ordinary file (the JSON text is written), 2 for the DATA MODE: c is a record as
*             $recarr makes it (an array of fields, each a string or an array of values, each of
*             those a string or an array of subvalues) and the record text is written; the types
*             on offer are string and array only, an array never below the second level, and
*             Ctrl-S refuses what $recarr cannot make a record of (message 12993).  With a null
*             id nothing is written and the changes are kept in c: that is how the IDE uses it
*     title   what the top line says the editor is editing
*     result  returned: <1> 1 if the record was saved (or c was kept), <2> 1 if the user asked
*             to leave the editor altogether (Ctrl-Q) so a caller with more records stops
*
*   The caller has the record locked with READU when fv is a file; the editor saves with
*   WRITEU and leaves the lock for the caller to release.
*
*   The screen: a title, the headings, one line for each element of the container being shown
*   (its name - or its number, for an array - its type and its value) in a table, the path of
*   the container, and a line for messages.  A '>' in the first column marks the selected element.
*   Questions (a value, a name, a position, a type, "save changes?") are asked in dialogues
*   over the list.  Without a terminal (a phantom) there is nothing to edit and the editor
*   returns at once.  An element whose name is empty or holds a slash cannot be reached by a
*   path, so it is shown and left alone; every other element can be edited.
*
*   A run time error would lose the user's work, so every name and position is checked before
*   it is used in a path.
*
*   THE SPLIT.  This program does the things that belong to a full screen of its own: the size check, the break setting, the screen, the painting, the key loop and the end
*   of the input, and the WRITE of the record (the edit gate first).  Everything else is $ced_core, which is given each key and answers in the state collection st (see its
*   header): the table, the path, the selection, the changed mark, the message, and req = 'save' when the record is to be written.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine ced_edit(c, fv, id, ftype, title, result)
$catalog $cedit

$include keyin.h
$include syscom keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include sl.h

   deffun edit_msg(emk) calling '!edit_msg'       ;* 18 Oct 26 dm, S.66

   LAYW = 140                         ;* what the screen is laid out for (a bigger window is used whole)
   LAYH = 43
   result = ''
   kind = jtype(c)
   if kind # 'collection' and kind # 'array' then return
   if ftype = 2 and kind # 'array' then return          ;* a record is an array of fields

   * A window smaller than the layout is refused (the library's one check and message); the caller with more records stops
   call $sl_need('CED', LAYW, LAYH, need.msg, '')
   if need.msg # '' then
      display need.msg
      result<2> = @true
      return
   end

   prompt ''
   * Ctrl-C is a key here (copy), so neither the break key nor the terminal's break character may act on
   * it; both are given back on the way out
   saved.trap = pterm(PT$BREAK, -1)
   call $tk_brk('save', saved.trap)         ;* 28 Oct 26: kept where $sl_cleanup can find it if the editor is aborted before it gives the setting back
   void pterm(PT$BREAK, @false)
   break off

   * The library's screen.  A session with no terminal has nothing to show an editor on.
   have.screen = @false
   screen open clear then
      have.screen = @true
   end else
      call $tk_brk('close', saved.trap)
      return
   end
   call $sl_guardmode('CED', LAYW, LAYH, 'layout', LAYW, LAYH)   ;* the window lock (layout mode, as the IDE): the keys are not the editor's while the WINDOW is smaller than LAYW by LAYH, wherever the cursor is
   call $tk_ai('usable', '', ai.ok, ai.pm)  ;* 24 Oct 26: F2 is the AI's key, only when the AI is usable for the account
   gosub build.keys

   gosub read.layout

   * The engine: its state is a collection, made here and kept for the life of the editor
   st = collection()
   st{'c'} = c
   st{'ftype'} = ftype
   st{'title'} = title
   st{'w'} = w
   st{'h'} = h
   st{'ai.ok'} = ai.ok
   st{'origin'} = 'none'                    ;* 1 Nov 26: the collection a calling program holds, not a record of any file: the AI's wall asks of the engine's host where a text comes from
   call $ced_core('init', st, '', out)
   gosub pull

   eof.count = 0

   loop
      gosub paint
      screen key returning c.key else c.key = ''
      gosub handle.key
   until done
   repeat

   screen close
   call $tk_brk('close', saved.trap)        ;* the setting back (or the earlier app's, if one was aborted) and BREAK ON; nothing is held any more
   result<1> = st{'kept'}
   result<2> = st{'quit.all'}
   return

* ======================================================================
* read.layout  -  w and h, the size the screen is laid out for: the window, and never less than LAYW by LAYH (a window made smaller
*                 after the start shows the upper left corner of the layout; the library cuts what falls outside)

read.layout:
   call $sl_window(w, h)
   if w < LAYW then w = LAYW
   if h < LAYH then h = LAYH
   return

* ======================================================================
* pull  -  What the painting and the key loop need from the engine's state.  c is read back every time: a revert or an accepted AI edit gives the engine a different one.

pull:
   c = st{'c'}
   path = st{'path'}
   sel = st{'sel'}
   top = st{'top'}
   count = st{'count'}
   tbl = st{'tbl'}
   dirty = st{'dirty'}
   message.text = st{'msg'}
   first.row = st{'first.row'}
   path.row = st{'path.row'}
   msg.row = st{'msg.row'}
   done = st{'done'}
   return

* ======================================================================
* Painting

paint:
   call $sl_scr('get', scr)
   ow = w
   oh = h
   gosub read.layout
   if w # ow or h # oh then
      * the terminal's window changed size: lay the screen out again
      call $ced_core('layout', st, w : @fm : h, out)
      gosub pull
   end
   call $sl_attr('bar', bc)
   t = sysmsg(12137, title)
   if dirty then t := ' *'
   call $sl_put(scr, 0, 0, fmt(t[1, w - 1], (w - 1) : 'L'), bc)
   tbl{'cur'} = sel
   tbl{'top'} = top
   call $sl_table('draw', tbl, scr, '', r)
   t = sysmsg(12143, '/' : change(path, @fm, '/'))
   call $sl_put(scr, 0, path.row, fmt(t[1, w - 1], (w - 1) : 'L'), 'n')
   t = message.text
   if t = '' then t = sysmsg(12144)
   call $sl_put(scr, 0, msg.row, fmt(t[1, w - 1], (w - 1) : 'L'), 'n')
   if count > 0 then
      scr<SL.S.CUR> = 0 : @vm : (first.row + sel - top)
   end else
      scr<SL.S.CUR> = 0 : @vm : first.row
   end
   call $sl_scr('put', scr)
   return

* ======================================================================
* handle.key  -  One key, in c.key (a name from the library: up, enter, f3, ctrl-s, or the character).  The engine does what the key means; when it asks for the
*                record to be written, it is written here.

handle.key:
   if c.key = '' then
      * End of input: the terminal has gone.  Give up without asking anything.
      eof.count += 1
      if eof.count > 20 then
         call $ced_core('abandon', st, '', out)
         gosub pull
      end
      return
   end
   eof.count = 0
   * 9 Nov 26 (S.181): the keys every page shares are this screen's, not the engine's
   begin case
      case c.key = 'ctrl-t'
         call $tk_std('theme', '', ck, '', tr)
         call $ced_core('message', st, tr<2>, out)
         gosub pull
         return
      case c.key = 'ctrl-k'
         call $tk_std('keylist', '', ck, '', tr)
         return
      case c.key = 'f5' or c.key = 'ctrl-l'
         call $sl_scr('get', scr)
         call $sl_repaint(scr)
         call $sl_scr('put', scr)
         return
   end case
   call $ced_core('key', st, c.key, out)
   gosub pull
   if st{'req'} = 'save' then gosub save.record
   return

* ======================================================================
* build.keys  -  9 Nov 26 (S.181): the table of every key this screen takes, for the keys list (Ctrl-K) and the shared rows ($tk_std): what each does, in the order help shows them

build.keys:
   ck = ''
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'f1', 'help', 'help', '', 1)
   if ai.ok then call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'f2', 'AI', 'ai', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'enter', 'open or change', 'dive', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'esc', 'up a level', 'up', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'f3', 'rename', 'rename', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'alt-t', 'type', 'type', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'ins', 'insert', 'insert', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'del', 'delete', 'delete', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'ctrl-c', 'copy', 'copy', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'ctrl-x', 'cut', 'cut', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'ctrl-v', 'paste', 'paste', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'f5', 'refresh', 'refresh', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'ctrl-s', 'save', 'save', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', ck, 'ctrl-q', 'quit', 'quit', '', 1)
   call $tk_std('keys', '', ck, '', tr)
   return

* ======================================================================
* Saving.  The engine makes the text (the data mode asks $recarr and may refuse) and takes the outcome; the gate and the write are here.

save.record:
   call $ced_core('prep.save', st, '', out)
   if not(st{'ok'}) then
      gosub pull                          ;* the message (12993) is the engine's; a quit that waited for this write does not go on
      return
   end
   rec.text = st{'text'}
   save.ok = @true
   save.status = 0
*  17 Oct 26 dm - S.64: CED is $internal, so the kernel's own test of a WRITE to a VOC, a dictionary or a program file lets it through;
*  it asks for itself, by the pathname of the open file.  The reason is the message (a DISPLAY would write over the screen).
*  Only for a record of a file: !CED() on a VARIABLE has no id and no file (fileinfo is null, which the kernel refuses by design).
   if id # '' then
      edit.p = fileinfo(fv, FL$PATH)
      if not(kernel(K$EDIT.OK, edit.p)) then
         edit.kind = kernel(K$EDIT.KIND, edit.p)
         call $ced_core('message', st, edit_msg(edit.kind), out)     ;* 12410 VOC, 12411 dictionary, 12412 program file, 12518 shared record, 12519 shared structure
         call $ced_core('saved', st, -1, out)
         gosub pull
         return
      end
   end
   if id # '' then                   ;* (fv is a file variable, which cannot be compared with a string)
      if ftype = 0 then
         writeu c on fv, id on error
            save.ok = @false
            save.status = status()
         end
      end else
         writeu rec.text on fv, id on error
            save.ok = @false
            save.status = status()
         end
      end
   end
   if save.ok then
      call $ced_core('saved', st, 1, out)
   end else
      call $ced_core('saved', st, 0 : @fm : save.status, out)
   end
   gosub pull
   return

end

* END-CODE
